WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.070
- Hey everyone, this is Luka.

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.120
I'm really excited to tell you
that my course on the basics

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.470
of R (for ecologist) is up and running.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:08.910
So if you're an ecologist and
you're just getting started

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.650
with R and feeling that
frustration of the learning curve

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:14.340
and just need something that
can take you by the hand to

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.180
really understand the
solid fundamentals of using

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:22.180
RforEcology, check out my course,
just go to RforEcology.com

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:26.585
there's a link also down below
and hope to see you there.

00:00:26.585 --> 00:00:29.335
(ethereal music)

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:34.960
Hey everyone, Luka here today,

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:38.320
I'm just gonna give you a
really short tutorial on the

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.350
basics of simple linear regression with R.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:43.680
So the most basic linear
regression models the

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.960
relationship between
two numeric variables.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.410
And so to begin with first,

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:51.870
we're going to load some data
and we'll just use the data

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:56.713
function in R, data to load
a data set called trees.

00:00:57.941 --> 00:00:59.790
And so when we run that line of code,

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:02.813
we get these trees data here.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.710
So these data represent the
measurements of the diameter,

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:09.970
height and volume of
31 black cherry trees.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.090
So note that girth is actually
the diameter breast height

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:15.100
of each tree measured in inches.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.840
Height is the tree height in
feet and volume is the volume

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.170
of the wood in cubic feet.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:22.920
So just for a little bit of clarity there,

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.120
let's rename the column
names of that data frame.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.310
And so we'll just do that by going names

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:33.920
so if we run the names function
that shows us what those

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.040
column names are, but within that,

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:38.500
we're going to replace those names with

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.913
DBH for diameter breast height in inches,

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:51.007
height in feet and volume in feet cubed.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:58.850
Alright, so now if we run that

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:02.130
and we take a look at
this data frame again,

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:06.070
we'll see that we've successfully
changed the column names.

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:09.950
Alright, so for the basic
linear regression let's model

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:14.840
how the tree diameter changes
as the tree grows taller.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:17.447
So first we're gonna start
out by writing what we

00:02:17.447 --> 00:02:19.820
actually want to model.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.793
So we want to know how DBH varies?

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.063
We want to know how DBH varies
as a function of tree height?

00:02:35.408 --> 00:02:37.400
And so we can write it out just like that,

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.557
and we can actually use these
column names to make it a

00:02:39.557 --> 00:02:41.330
little bit more specific.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:44.050
So how does DBH and inches vary

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:46.403
as a function of tree height?

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:50.210
And note that I use this
little squiggly sign here,

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:53.160
the Tilde or Tilde on my keyboard.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:54.700
I'm not sure where it is on all keyboards,

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.740
but on my keyboard, it's
just under the Escape key

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.700
on the top left part of the keyboard.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.010
And I hold down Shift
and press that key under

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:06.710
the Escape key to get this Tilde symbol,

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:07.794
and you'll see why it's important

00:03:07.794 --> 00:03:10.130
to know about this Tilde
symbol in a moment,

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:12.280
but you can read this symbol as

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:17.433
is a function of.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:21.120
So now we can look at this
statement right here and say,

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.080
DBH in inches is a
function of height in feet.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.000
And so we can think of our
hypothesis for this regression as

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.260
being this model that
we've just come up with.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.940
Another way to think
about it is DBH in inches

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:38.660
is the Y variable, height
in feet is the X variable.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:42.860
And so the Y variable or DBH
in inches is also the dependent

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:44.320
or the response variable.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.420
And so we can say, how does the Y variable

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:50.970
depend or respond to the X variable?

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.460
Now, it's also important to
note that we are not drawing any

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.640
conclusions about the causal relationship

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:57.770
between DBH and tree height.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:00.338
The linear regression analysis
simply allows us to test the

00:04:00.338 --> 00:04:03.840
correlation or association
of these two variables,

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:07.400
but it doesn't really tell us
about what is affecting what.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:10.700
So let's begin by
visualizing the relationship

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.490
of DBH and height.

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:15.460
And so the neat thing is we
can use this formula here

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:19.150
this is a function of this
to make the plot as well.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:22.540
So we'll just use the plot
function and then we'll say,

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.440
DBH in inches is a function of,

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:26.880
whenever I say is a function of,

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.733
it'll refer to this Tilde symbol.

00:04:29.633 --> 00:04:33.040
DBH in inches is a
function of height in feet.

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:40.260
And then we'll add one more argument here

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.470
indicating the data set
that this comes from

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:44.053
so the data frame.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:49.680
Data equals trees.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:54.000
And so let's run this line
of code and there we have it.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.031
A simple plot showing the
relationship between DBH

00:04:58.031 --> 00:05:01.590
on the Y axis, remember
DBH was the Y variable here

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:03.980
and height in feet on the X axis.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:07.540
So as height changes, so does DBH.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:08.980
Apparently so it looks like there might be

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:11.270
some kind of positive relationship there,

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:13.940
but that's what the
linear regression is for.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:15.310
Let's see if there is actually

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:17.020
a significant relationship there.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:19.100
Also actually, before we move on,

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:21.170
I'm not a big fan of these open circles,

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:22.970
I think it looks a little
better when the circles are

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:25.380
closed, unless you have a
lot of overlapping points.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.500
For example, here we have
two overlapping points,

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:29.950
which might be harder to see
if the circles are filled in,

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:32.680
but I think that's a small
enough sacrifice to make this

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:33.970
plot look a little better.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:36.633
So we'll just add in one
more argument here, going.

00:05:36.633 --> 00:05:41.136
Pch equals 16 and that'll just
make the points filled in.

00:05:41.136 --> 00:05:44.400
There we go, so to do
the regression analysis,

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.720
all we have to do is actually
take the contents of this plot

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:52.440
function and put it inside an lm function,

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:53.890
which stands for linear mode.

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:57.603
So we'll just copy this.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:01.260
And we also have to refer to the data

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.396
so we'll add this in as well.

00:06:03.396 --> 00:06:05.110
And we're not actually plotting anything,

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:08.600
so we don't need this pch parameter here.

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:10.443
But now if we run this,

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:14.470
we get some of the results of the model.

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:17.340
We actually get the estimate
for the coefficient,

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:22.340
for the intercept and the slope
as it's affected by height

00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:23.850
in this model.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:25.950
So it's not a lot to work with yet,

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:29.130
but we can save this as its own objects,

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:30.843
so we'll just call it nod,

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:36.753
and then we can apply the
summary function to mod.

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:39.100
And when we run that,

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:42.060
we get some more interesting
information here.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:45.890
So first we can see the
distribution of the residuals or the

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:48.490
unexplained variation in the model.

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:50.550
With the minimum value of the residual,

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:53.590
the maximum, the median,
and the first quartile

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:55.700
and third quartile range.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:57.810
But below that, it gets more interesting.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:06:59.671
Here, we have a table of the coefficient.

00:06:59.671 --> 00:07:02.689
So again, before without
running summary function,

00:07:02.689 --> 00:07:06.430
we just got this value for
the intercept coefficient

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:08.790
and the slope coefficient,

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:11.030
how height is associated with DBH.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:14.500
Then in this second column,
we have the standard error

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:18.410
around that estimate of the coefficient.

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.930
Then we have the test
statistic in this case,

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:23.760
T value for each of these estimates.

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:25.540
And finally, maybe what everyone's really

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:28.680
wanting to look for is the P value

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:32.650
or the significance of
each of these coefficients.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:35.220
So to think about these
coefficients and what the

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.460
significance means, think of it as

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:41.090
what a linear model actually is.

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:44.897
Linear model is simply Y = a + bX.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:53.530
So to model a straight line
or linear line as described

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:57.490
by a linear model, you
need two coefficients.

00:07:57.490 --> 00:08:01.144
First, you have this intercept
value or the A in this case,

00:08:01.144 --> 00:08:05.260
which represents where the
line crosses over zero.

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:09.930
And then you have this B
coefficient or the slope

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:13.020
coefficient, which is multiplied by X

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:15.300
to show you the rate at which that line

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:19.700
or the angle at which that
line changes with respect to X.

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:22.940
So what a linear regression
actually does is it tries to

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:26.730
optimize the value of the
intercept and the slope

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:30.640
to create a line that best
describes this relationship

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:33.430
of points between height and DBH.

00:08:33.430 --> 00:08:37.330
And then we can use a
statistical test to see if these

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:40.960
coefficients are actually
significantly different from zero.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:43.730
So after this little table
here we have a little bit

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:47.340
more information, here we've
got these significant codes,

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:50.400
which are just these asterisk
symbols placed at the end of

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:53.170
the P value to quickly
visualize which of these

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:55.270
might be statistically significant.

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:58.080
But then down here, we have
the residual standard error,

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:00.850
which indicates the variation
of unexplained variance

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:03.100
around the regression line.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:05.940
Then we have the Multiple R-squared,

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:09.660
which can be thought of as
the proportion of variance

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:13.510
in the data that can be
explained by the model.

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:16.650
So you can ignore the
Adjusted R-squared for now,

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:17.750
if you're just starting out,

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:19.890
maybe I'll talk about
that in a future lesson.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:22.800
And then finally we have the
F-statistic and P value that

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:25.980
tests whether all the coefficients
in the model are zero.

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:30.490
So next, let's actually add
this best fit line to this plot

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:33.590
since we can see that height
is actually a significant

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:36.380
coefficient, excuse me,
the slope is a significant

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:38.900
coefficient suggesting that
height is significantly

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:41.230
associated with diameter breast height.

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:43.283
So let's go ahead and plot that line.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.423
Just go back to our script here.

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:49.210
And so to add a line to this model,

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:53.610
all you have to do is run the
original plot that we created.

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:55.433
So I'll just run this one more time.

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:03.050
And then add in the function abline.

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:05.836
Remember the A and the B that I was using

00:10:05.836 --> 00:10:09.050
to explain how linear model works.

00:10:09.050 --> 00:10:13.670
So abline uses a slope and an
intercept to create a line.

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:15.895
And abline actually will take

00:10:15.895 --> 00:10:20.895
the model that we had
fit as its main argument

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:24.120
and create the line
from those coefficients.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:27.080
So there it is, that is the best fit line

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:28.430
through these points.

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:30.730
So finally, let's say
that you need to extract

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:33.360
a table with the regression results.

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:35.990
So here's a cool trick for
quickly extracting a neat table

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:38.490
using the package called broom.

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:43.100
So first you need to install.packages

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:49.700
and run this if you haven't
installed broom yet.

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:51.180
If you have installed it,

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:52.590
you don't ever have to run it again,

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:55.074
unless you re-install R on a new machine

00:10:55.074 --> 00:10:58.530
or uninstall your
packages for some reason.

00:10:58.530 --> 00:10:59.962
So I'm just gonna command that out,

00:10:59.962 --> 00:11:02.860
but after you finished installing it,

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:07.860
run library broom to load up that package.

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:13.270
So you have to run the library
function each time you want

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:15.120
to load up a package, but the install is

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:18.500
only the very first time you
install it on your machine.

00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:21.000
And then we're gonna use
this function called tidy

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:26.450
to create a table of the
results to extract a neat table

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:28.370
of the results from our model.

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:32.490
And so now the argument will
just be mod again, our model.

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:35.810
And so note that this table
is actually called A tibble,

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:37.720
which is better than a normal data frame,

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:40.450
but that's for another lesson.

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:41.790
So thanks for watching this video,

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:43.660
if you liked it and would
like to see more like it

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:45.230
just hit subscribe below.

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:47.040
If you want to see my full
courses on R (for ecologists),

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:50.210
just see the link below and otherwise,

00:11:50.210 --> 00:11:53.303
I hope you have a
fantastic rest of your day.

